' bug-484: the governing rule for every kind of name — a name defined LOCALLY
' needs no prefix, a name that is IMPORTED requires one.
'
' Both halves are pinned here, in one file, because the bug was that they had
' drifted apart: `src/resolver/mod.rs`'s seed list of always-resolvable type
' names held six BARE leaves (`Address`, `Datagram`, `TermColor`, `TermSize`,
' `AudioDevice`, `Json`) where every other entry was a package-qualified id. A
' bare leaf there makes that one name resolvable from ANY file with no prefix,
' so `AS Address` compiled from a consumer while the sibling `AS Url` — same
' package, same kind, but absent from the list — was correctly refused. Nothing
' failed to build; the rule simply stopped applying to six names.
'
' The local declarations are load-bearing, not scenery. A local name must keep
' winning bare (E, G), including when it shadows an imported one (E shadows
' net::Url) — otherwise "fix the bare-name hole" would have meant refusing every
' local type too, and adding an import would break unrelated code.
'
' Note what the rule rules OUT: there is no "ambiguous" outcome for a type name.
' A bare name means the local one, so it either resolves to a local declaration
' or it is undefined. How many imported packages happen to export the same leaf
' never enters into it — which is why C below is a not-defined error even though
' both tcp and udp export a `Socket`.
IMPORT net
IMPORT tcp
IMPORT udp
IMPORT tls

TYPE Url                    ' Local Url
    u as String
END TYPE

TYPE Foo                    ' Local Foo
    bar as String
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareA
    addr AS Address         ' Should fail (not defined)
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareB
    addr AS net::Address    ' Should not fail (net)
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareC
    s AS RES Socket         ' Should fail (not defined) — bare means LOCAL, and
                            ' there is no local Socket. That tcp, udp and tls all
                            ' export one is irrelevant: none of them are in scope
                            ' bare, so this is undefined, NOT ambiguous. Ambiguity
                            ' is not a category here — a bare name has exactly one
                            ' meaning, the local one.
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareD
    s AS RES tcp::Socket    ' Should not fail (tcp)
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareE
    s AS RES Listener       ' Should fail (not defined) — bare means LOCAL, and
                            ' there is no local Listener. That tcp and tls both
                            ' export one is irrelevant: neither is in scope bare,
                            ' so this is undefined, NOT ambiguous. Ambiguity is
                            ' not a category here — a bare name has exactly one
                            ' meaning, the local one.
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareF
    u AS Url                ' Should not fail (local)
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareG
    u AS net::Url           ' Should not fail (net)
END TYPE

TYPE TestBareH
    u AS Foo                ' Should not fail (local)
END TYPE

FUNC main AS Integer
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
